.\" vi:set wm=5
.TH IMG2SIXEL 1 "Aug 2016"
.if n .ds Q \&"
.if n .ds U \&"
.if t .ds Q ``
.if t .ds U ''
.UC 4
.SH NAME
img2sixel \- image converter to DEC SIXEL graphics


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B img2sixel
[ \-\fIoptions\fP ] \fIimagefiles\fP
.br
.B img2sixel
[ \-\fIoptions\fP ] < \fIimagefiles\fP
.ta .5i 1.8i


.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIimg2sixel\fP converts various images into high quality DEC SIXEL image format.
.PP
The converter recognises the pseudo file target \(dqclipboard:\(dq in
addition to traditional paths.  Reading from \(dqclipboard:\(dq pulls the
most recent image or UTF-8 string payload from the desktop clipboard, while
writing to \(dqclipboard:\(dq publishes the generated SIXEL stream as
plain text.  Prefixes such as \(dqpng:clipboard:\(dq request that an
intermediate PNG snapshot is written to the clipboard instead of the raw
SIXEL sequence.  When reading, the converter probes for PNG data first,
falls back to TIFF, and finally consumes UTF-8 strings if no image payload
is present.

.SS "Network input"
Input operands containing \(dq://\(dq are passed to the compiled network
bindings, in this order: WinHTTP, libcurl, then libfetch. The first successful
fetch is used; a failed attempt can fall back to the next compiled binding.
The \fB-L\fP option selects image decoders, not network bindings.
The fetched resource must still contain a supported image format.
.PP
WinHTTP accepts HTTP and HTTPS. libcurl can support additional schemes such
as FTP, FTPS, file, SFTP, and SCP, depending on its version, build options,
and linked TLS or SSH backend. Native libfetch provides HTTP, FTP, and file
access, with HTTPS dependent on its implementation and TLS-enabled build.
The Emscripten libfetch option uses the runtime Fetch API instead of BSD
libfetch and remains subject to runtime access restrictions.
.PP
Use \fB--version\fP to see which bindings were compiled in. Their presence
does not enumerate all supported schemes or identify the successful binding.
Even file:// input requires a suitable binding; an ordinary local path does
not. Redirects, proxies, and certificate trust configuration are
backend-dependent. See \fB-k\fP for the certificate-verification bypass.


.SS "OpenVMS DCL"
When invoking this program as a DCL foreign command, preserve the case of
option letters, compact suboption letters and case-sensitive values.
The default DCL/C runtime argument processing lowercases unquoted
arguments. Enclose arguments whose uppercase characters must be preserved
in double quotes:
.PP
.nf
img2sixel \(dq\-Wc\(dq \(dq\-Qk\(dq input.png
.fi
.PP
This applies whether or not an argument contains a slash.
Lowercase arguments do not require quoting solely to preserve their case;
foreign-command invocation does not require every argument to be quoted.
Use DCL double quotes, rather than POSIX-shell single quotes.


.SH "COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS"
\fIimg2sixel\fP has the following command-line options:
.PP
Arguments that select from a fixed set accept unique prefixes.  For
example, "\-s ave" resolves to "average", but "\-s a" is rejected because
it matches both "auto" and "average".
." Keep option signatures synchronized with `img2sixel -H`; grouping may differ.
.TP 5
.B \-o, \-\-outfile
specify output file name (default:stdout).
Selecting a name ending in \(dq.png\(dq, the literal \(dqpng:-\(dq, or the
prefix \(dqpng:\(dq emits PNG data that reproduces the generated SIXEL
stream. For ordinary palette SIXEL, the PNG keeps the palette indices
and mirrors the quantized and dithered output. PNG selection preserves
the ordinary native float palette path and the byte fast path.
Named \fB\-U\fP destinations record their RGB ICC profile; legacy
output definitions retain the existing sRGB interpretation. In particular,
linear and SMPTE-C snapshots are numeric previews, not matching-profile
renditions. Native snapshots exclude macro/DRCS envelopes and OR mode.
PNG snapshots conflict with \fB-Z delta\fP because their decoder has no
retained base image. Select \fB-Z full\fP before the PNG destination.
High-color output is not
an indexed-exact snapshot because it redefines palette registers while
painting. \(dqpng:-\(dq writes to standard output, while
\(dqpng:\fIpath\fP\(dq stores the PNG alongside the SIXEL output. Using
\(dq\-o output.png\(dq writes the PNG directly to that path.
.TP 5
.B \-= \fIPOLICY\fP[:\fIKEY\fP=\fIVALUE\fP...], \-\-threading\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP[:\fIKEY\fP=\fIVALUE\fP...]
plan where worker threads go.
\fIPOLICY\fP selects budget spending: \fBlazy\fP overlaps independent
branches and pipelines with one default dither band; \fBbalanced\fP
uses size heuristics and caps the budget to performance logical CPUs on
hybrid hardware (default); \fBgreedy\fP uses the full budget and ignores
colorspace_min, sample_min and scale_min_bytes, including explicit -j values.
Explicit stage keys override mode defaults.
Stage limits that exceed the budget shrink to fit it; they are never an
error.
With \fBpalette=frame\fP, frame keys and \fBprepare_workers\fP preserve
output; the dither worker count selects the parallel band split. With
\fBpalette=latest\fP, scheduling can change the selected palette.
.RS
.TP 5
.B global_budget=COUNT|all
positive total thread budget or case-insensitive \fBall\fP (short form
\fB:Ball\fP). All selects logical CPUs, subject to the balanced
performance-core cap. The converter defaults to all; the library defaults
to min(4, performance CPUs) in every mode. Applies to encoding and decoding.
Precedence is the CLI key, then sixel_set_encoder_threads(), then the environment,
then defaults. A nonpositive API count means all.
.TP 5
.B load=serial|ahead
decode frames ahead of encoding (default \fBahead\fP; short form \fB:L\fP).
.TP 5
.B load_depth=1..16
frames decoded ahead (default 4; short form \fB:Q\fP).
.TP 5
.B prepare=serial|ahead
prepare frame n+1 while frame n is emitted (default \fBahead\fP; short form
\fB:P\fP).
.TP 5
.B palette=frame|latest|auto
palette source of each frame (default \fBframe\fP; short form \fB:C\fP).
\fBlatest\fP selects the newest completed palette from the current or an
earlier frame at application start, after the display delay. Selection is
fixed throughout that frame, beginning before LUT/gradient setup. Completed
current-frame palettes are eligible; future-frame palettes never are. Output
depends on completion timing and can differ between repeated runs. There is
no scene-change detector. Ordinary CPU animations and public playback
can overlap their own palette build with application; the first frame waits.
A one-worker budget, nonthreaded execution, still images, fixed/indexed/high-color
palettes, GPU-enabled paths, DRCS, presentation/repair and generated macros
retain \fBframe\fP behavior.
Delta/alpha reservation or color-profile mismatch waits for the current
frame's own palette; stale prepare-ahead predictions are joined and rebuilt.
Native color output and palette export use the selected palette. A frame's own build
failure is reported even if output was already emitted. Serial fallbacks match
\fBframe\fP output bytes with the same options.
Latest emission keeps the full encoder budget. Its palette builders borrow
idle execution slots; ordinary next preparation retains its independent cap.
Load/prepare serial settings alone do not disable latest.
\fBauto\fP starts in frame behavior and probes latest only after palette
construction delays emission and five intervals exist. Each probe uses five
ABBA blocks (20 frames), A the incumbent and B the challenger. Each block
averages adjacent pair contrasts 2*(A-B)/(A+B); the median of the five
contrasts must exceed 0.03 plus twice their median absolute deviation to
switch. Balanced orders reduce content-trend bias; five blocks tolerate two
outliers. The 3% floor guards small repeat variation, and twice MAD adds an
empirical noise allowance, not a confidence interval. Uncertain or incomplete
probes retain the choice without back-off. Reevaluation starts after 40 chosen
frames; decisive incumbent confirmations double that interval up to 320,
while reversals reset it to 40. A no-wait frame reevaluation skips the probe
and backs off. Completed compatible palettes survive frame periods.
Each probe schedules ten frames of each behavior. Intervals run from one frame's
output, own-build join and cleanup completion to the next such completion,
including writes and preparation waits. The first completion establishes the
origin. Interior probe modes are passed to prepare-ahead without a hold;
only the final decision defers next-frame preparation. Probe and transition
costs can outweigh a benefit; no speedup is guaranteed. The default remains
\fBframe\fP.
.TP 5
.B dither=COUNT
palette application workers (short form \fB:D\fP).
.TP 5
.B encode=COUNT
SIXEL encode workers (short form \fB:E\fP).
.TP 5
.B prepare_workers=COUNT
scale, colorspace, sampling and palette construction workers (short form
\fB:W\fP).
.TP 5
.B pin=0|1
bind workers to CPUs; dither workers default to 1 and FHEDT workers to 0
(short form \fB:I\fP).
.RE
.IP
Compact suboption names use one uppercase letter plus the value:
global_budget=B, load=L, load_depth=Q, prepare=P, palette=C, dither=D, encode=E,
prepare_workers=W, decode=S, pin=I.
.IP
When \fBSIXEL_ENCODER_THREADING_POLICY\fP is unset or empty, the \fBimg2sixel\fP
frontend defaults to \fBbalanced\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-. \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-precision\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
select the sample precision of palette application.
.RS
.TP 5
.B auto
-> promote to float32 where CMS output, high-depth source samples,
   color-space calculations or the clustering view require it (default).
.TP 5
.B 8bit
-> apply the palette on 8-bit gamma samples. Earlier stages may still
   use float32; the work samples and the palette are rounded to bytes
   just before palette application. A non-gamma \fB-W\fP and a named
   \fB-U\fP destination are rejected.
.TP 5
.B float32
-> request float processing even when byte storage would suffice.
.RE
.TP 5
.B \-G \fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP, \-\-gpu\-policy=\fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP
choose the upper-level GPU accelerator policy.
\fBoff\fP keeps all stages on CPU (default); \fBauto\fP tries eligible
GPU stages with CPU fallback; \fBforce\fP requires supported GPU execution
for selected GPU stages and palette application.
K-means additionally requires \fB-Qkmeans:backend=gpu\fP.
The encoder suboption \fB:palette_threshold=PIXELS\fP, with short form
\fB:PPIXELS\fP, sets the minimum image size used by \fBauto\fP
for palette application only, without gating GPU k-means.
.TP 5
.B \-j \fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP, \-\-runtime\-policy=\fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP
choose process-wide runtime tuning.  \fIPOLICY\fP is \fBauto\fP,
\fBnone/scalar\fP, \fBsse2\fP, \fBavx\fP, or \fBneon\fP and limits the SIMD
implementation selected at runtime.
Encoder suboptions are \fB:colorspace_min=PIXELS\fP
(short form \fB:CPIXELS\fP), \fB:sample_min=PIXELS\fP
(\fB:SPIXELS\fP), \fB:parallel_factor=ROWS\fP
(\fB:FROWS\fP), \fB:resize_precision=preserve|linear|float\fP
(\fB:Rvalue\fP), and \fB:scale_min_bytes=BYTES\fP
(\fB:BBYTES\fP).
.TP 5
.B \-x \fIMODE[:KEY=VALUE]\fP, \-\-diagnostics=\fIMODE[:KEY=VALUE]\fP
choose diagnostic presentation.  \fIMODE\fP is \fBhuman\fP (default) or
\fBcode\fP.  Common suboptions are \fB:prefix_suggestions=0|1\fP
(\fB:P0|:P1\fP), \fB:fuzzy_suggestions=0|1\fP
(\fB:F0|:F1\fP), \fB:path_suggestions=0|1\fP
(\fB:S0|:S1\fP), and \fB:force_colors=0|1\fP
(\fB:C0|:C1\fP).  \fB:trace_topic=LIST\fP
(\fB:TLIST\fP) selects a comma-separated topic list.
img2sixel also accepts \fB:quiet=0|1\fP
(\fB:Q0|:Q1\fP), \fB:handoff_trace=0|1\fP (\fB:H0|:H1\fP),
\fB:psd_trace=0|1\fP (\fB:D0|:D1\fP), and
\fB:psd_header_only=0|1\fP (\fB:E0|:E1\fP). Abort tracing uses
\fB:abort_trace=0|1\fP (\fB:A0|:A1\fP). Timeline line sampling uses
\fB:log_lines=1..2147483647\fP (short form \fB:NVALUE\fP).
.TP 5
.B \-J \fIPATH\fP, \-\-log\-path=\fIPATH\fP
write the shared JSON timeline log to \fIPATH\fP. This option takes
precedence over \fBSIXEL_LOG_PATH\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-y \fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP, \-\-clipboard\-policy=\fIPOLICY[:KEY=VALUE]\fP
choose the clipboard backend. \fIPOLICY\fP is \fBsystem\fP (default) or
\fBfile\fP. The file backend accepts \fB:directory=PATH\fP
(short form \fB:DPATH\fP). CLI values take precedence over
\fBSIXEL_CLIPBOARD_BACKEND\fP and \fBSIXEL_CLIPBOARD_FILE_DIR\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-z \fI0|1\fP, \-\-terminal\-policy=\fI0|1\fP
control whether animated tty output hides the terminal cursor with DECTCEM.
The library default is \fB0\fP. \fIimg2sixel\fP uses \fB1\fP when neither
this option nor \fBSIXEL_ANIMATION_HIDE_CURSOR\fP is configured.
.TP 5
.B \-7, \-\-7bit-mode
generate a sixel image for 7bit terminals or printers (default).
.TP 5
.B \-8, \-\-8bit-mode
generate a sixel image for 8bit terminals or printers.
.TP 5
.B \-R, \-\-gri-limit
limit arguments of DECGRI('!') to 255.
.TP 5
.B \-p \fICOLORS\fP, \-\-colors=\fICOLORS\fP
specify number of colors to reduce the image to (default=256).
.TP 5
.B \-Q \fIMODEL\fP, \-\-quantize\-model=\fIMODEL\fP
select the palette solver. \fIMODEL\fP accepts \fBauto\fP,
\fBheckbert\fP, \fBkmeans\fP, \fBmedoids\fP, or \fBcenter\fP. The
\fBauto\fP setting preserves
the historical Heckbert median\-cut behaviour.
.br
Long suboption keys use \fBkey=value\fP. Uppercase short keys use
\fBKvalue\fP without an equals sign, for example \fBkmeans:Ipca\fP.
.br
Every base accepts \fBsample_target\fP (short form \fBCCOUNT\fP), a
positive palette sampling count.
.br
The \fBcenter\fP base value accepts suboptions using
\fBcenter:key=value[:key=value...]\fP.
.br
Supported k-center suboptions:
.br
\fBalgo\fP (short form \fBANAME\fP): \fBauto\fP, \fBfft\fP, \fBswap\fP,
or \fBhybrid\fP.
.br
\fBprofile\fP: \fBlegacy\fP, \fBspeed\fP, \fBbalance\fP, or
\fBquality\fP.
.br
\fBauto_policy\fP: \fBlegacy\fP or \fBadaptive\fP.
.br
\fBauto_fft_threshold\fP: integer value in 256-65536.
.br
\fBspace_policy\fP: \fBlegacy\fP or \fBperceptual\fP.
.br
\fBcandidate_policy\fP: \fBlegacy\fP or \fBhybrid\fP.
.br
\fBseed\fP (short form \fBSVALUE\fP): integer value in 0-4294967295.
.br
\fBrestarts\fP: integer value in 1-32.
.br
\fBinit_seeds\fP: integer value in 1-8.
.br
\fBiter\fP: integer value in 1-64.
.br
\fBhistbits\fP: integer value in 3-6.
.br
\fBpoint_budget\fP: 0 or integer value in 64-16384.
.br
\fBrare_keep\fP: 0 or integer value in 1-2048.
.br
\fBprune_mass\fP: floating value in 0.900-1.000.
.br
\fBbudget_policy\fP: \fBlegacy\fP or \fBadaptive\fP.
.br
\fBbudget_scale\fP: floating value in 0.25-4.00.
.br
\fBswap_topk\fP: integer value in 1-16.
.br
\fBswap_update\fP: \fBfull\fP or \fBincremental\fP.
.br
\fBswap_patience\fP: 0 or integer value in 1-8.
.br
\fBswap_min_gain\fP: floating value in 0.0-8.0.
.br
The \fBkmeans\fP base value accepts suboptions using
\fBkmeans:key=value[:key=value...]\fP.
.br
Supported k-means suboptions:
.br
\fBbackend\fP (short form \fBGNAME\fP): \fBcpu\fP (default) or \fBgpu\fP.
GPU Lloyd iteration requires a Metal-enabled build and an available device.
It uses FP32 arithmetic and full center scans; \fBprune\fP controls CPU
execution. Sampling, initialization, final assignment and merge remain on CPU.
With \fB--gpu-policy=auto\fP, residual feedback, active iteration snap,
unsupported platforms, resource/numeric limits and failed GPU attempts fall
back to CPU from unchanged seeds. With \fBforce\fP they return an error. Supported requests have at most 1048576
weighted points and 1024 internal centers, finite coordinates within
+/-65536 and nonnegative weights no larger than 4294967295; nonzero
coordinates and weights must be representable as normal FP32 values.
Results need not be bit-identical to CPU double arithmetic.
\fB--gpu-policy=off\fP (default) keeps k-means on CPU even when
\fBbackend=gpu\fP is selected. \fBbackend=cpu\fP stays on CPU under
every GPU policy. \fBforce\fP also retains its palette-application
requirement; for example, use \fB--lookup-policy=none -dnone\fP.
.br
\fBinittype\fP (short form \fBITYPE\fP): \fBauto\fP, \fBnone\fP, \fBpca\fP.
.br
\fBthreshold\fP (short form \fBTVALUE\fP): floating value in 0.0-0.5.
.br
\fBbinbits\fP (short form \fBNBITS\fP): integer value in 4-8.
.br
\fBmapping\fP (short form \fBMSPACE\fP): \fBuniform\fP or \fBsrgb\fP.
.br
\fBsoftdist\fP (short form \fBDKIND\fP): \fBtrilinear\fP.
.br
\fBfeedback\fP (short form \fBF0\fP or \fBF1\fP): 0 or 1.
.br
\fBprune\fP: \fBauto\fP, \fBnone\fP, \fBhamerly\fP, \fBelkan\fP, or
\fByinyang\fP.
.br
\fBseed\fP (short form \fBSVALUE\fP): integer value in 0-4294967295.
.br
\fBrestarts\fP: integer value in 1-32.
.br
\fBiter\fP: integer value in 1-100. Takes precedence over \fBiter_max\fP
when both are specified.
.br
\fBiter_max\fP: integer value in 1-100.
.br
\fBminiter\fP: 0 or integer value in 1-100.
.br
\fBpolish_iter\fP: 0 or integer value in 1-16.
.br
\fBfeedback_slots\fP: integer value in 1-16.
.br
\fBfeedback_interval\fP: integer value in 1-64.
.br
The \fBmedoids\fP base value accepts suboptions using
\fBmedoids:key=value[:key=value...]\fP.
.br
Supported k-medoids suboptions:
.br
\fBalgo\fP (short form \fBANAME\fP): \fBauto\fP, \fBpam\fP, \fBsample\fP
(CLARA), \fBrandom\fP (CLARANS), or \fBbandit\fP (BanditPAM).
.br
\fBseed\fP (short form \fBSVALUE\fP): integer value in 0-4294967295.
.br
\fBiter\fP: integer value in 1-64.
.br
\fBsample\fP: 0 or integer value in 64-1048576.
.br
\fBpoint_budget\fP: integer value in 64-16384.
.br
\fBhistbits\fP: integer value in 3-6.
.br
\fBrare_keep\fP: integer value in 0-1024.
.br
\fBprune_mass\fP: floating value in 0.900-1.000.
.br
\fBauction\fP: 0 or 1 (default 0).
.br
\fBauction_shortlist\fP: integer value in 2-8 (default 4).
.br
\fBclara_trials\fP: integer value in 1-32.
.br
\fBclara_sample\fP: 0 or integer value in 64-1048576.
.br
\fBclarans_local\fP: integer value in 1-32.
.br
\fBclarans_neighbors\fP: 0 or integer value in 1-5000000.
.br
\fBbandit_iter\fP: integer value in 1-64.
.br
\fBbandit_candidates\fP: integer value in 8-4096.
.br
\fBbandit_batch\fP: integer value in 8-4096.
.br
Compact suboption names use one uppercase letter followed by the value:
.br
all models: sample_target=C;
.br
heckbert: profile=P, cut=median|sse (T),
order=population|sse (O), weights=auto|L/A/B (W);
.br
kmeans: backend=G, inittype=I, threshold=T, binbits=N, mapping=M, softdist=D,
feedback=F, prune=P, seed=S, restarts=E,
iter=A, iter_max=X, miniter=U, polish_iter=H, feedback_slots=K,
feedback_interval=J;
.br
medoids: algo=A, seed=S, iter=I, sample=M, clara_trials=T,
clara_sample=K, clarans_local=J, clarans_neighbors=N, bandit_iter=D,
bandit_candidates=E, bandit_batch=X, histbits=H, point_budget=B,
rare_keep=R, prune_mass=U, auction=Q, auction_shortlist=Y;
.br
center: algo=A, profile=P, seed=S, auto_policy=Q,
auto_fft_threshold=F, space_policy=E, candidate_policy=Z, restarts=X,
init_seeds=N, iter=I, histbits=H, point_budget=B, rare_keep=R,
prune_mass=U, budget_policy=D, budget_scale=Y, swap_topk=K,
swap_update=M, swap_patience=T, swap_min_gain=J.
.TP 5
.B \-4 \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-sampling\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
choose which pixels feed palette construction.
\fIPOLICY\fP accepts \fBauto\fP, \fBfull\-frame\fP, or
\fBadaptive\-grid\fP. \fBfull\-frame\fP uses the preprocessed output
frame. \fBadaptive\-grid\fP samples the configured crop range directly from
loaded-frame coordinates before resize and colorspace preprocessing. Thread
availability may overlap adaptive sampling with other work, but does not
select the sampling policy. \fBauto\fP resolves to
\fBadaptive\-grid\fP at every thread count, including one thread.
The \fBSIXEL_SAMPLING_POLICY\fP environment provides the default.
.TP 5
.B \-5 \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-binning\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
choose how sampled colors become weighted quantizer input.
\fIPOLICY\fP accepts \fBauto\fP, \fBnone\fP, \fBexact\fP,
\fBhard\fP, or \fBsoft\fP. \fBnone\fP preserves the raw sample sequence;
\fBexact\fP combines only identical colors at full precision; \fBhard\fP
assigns each color to one finite grid bin; and \fBsoft\fP distributes its mass
to neighboring bins with trilinear weights. Explicit \fBexact\fP,
\fBhard\fP, or \fBsoft\fP selects k-means when \fB\-Q auto\fP is active.
\fBauto\fP selects hard binning for a compatible weighted-point quantizer;
other quantizers select a compatible input form.
The \fBSIXEL_BINNING_POLICY\fP environment provides the default.
.TP 5
.B \-F \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-merge\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
configure final palette merging independently of the quantizer.
\fIPOLICY\fP accepts \fBauto\fP, \fBnone\fP, or \fBward\fP.
.br
Supported suboptions:
.br
\fBmerge_oversplit\fP (short form \fBOFACTOR\fP): 1.0-3.0.
.br
\fBmerge_lloyd\fP (short form \fBLCOUNT\fP): integer value in 0-30.
.br
\fBchannel_l\fP (short form \fBCFACTOR\fP): floating value in 0.0-1.0.
.br
The corresponding environments are \fBSIXEL_PALETTE_FINAL_MERGE\fP,
\fBSIXEL_PALETTE_OVERSPLIT_FACTOR\fP,
\fBSIXEL_PALETTE_FINAL_MERGE_ADDITIONAL_LLOYD_ITER_COUNT\fP, and
\fBSIXEL_PALETTE_MERGE_CHANNEL_FACTOR_L\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-a \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-cover\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
configure palette anchoring independently of the quantizer.
\fIPOLICY\fP accepts \fBauto\fP, \fBoff\fP, \fBcorners\fP,
\fBfaces\fP, \fBedges\fP, or \fBall\fP.
\fBauto\fP disables repair for float palettes; byte palettes select by
actual palette size. Mode or growth suboptions alone do not enable float
AUTO repair; select an explicit anchor budget to enable it.
.br
Supported suboptions:
.br
\fBcover_grow\fP (short form \fBV0\fP or \fBV1\fP): 0 or 1.
.br
\fBcover_mode\fP (short form \fBWMODE\fP): \fBsoft\fP or \fBhard\fP.
.br
The corresponding environments are \fBSIXEL_PALETTE_COVER\fP,
\fBSIXEL_PALETTE_COVER_GROW\fP, and \fBSIXEL_PALETTE_COVER_MODE\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-_ \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-snap\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
control whether and how palette channels approach the 101 reversible SIXEL
tone levels independently of palette coverage. \fBnone\fP disables snapping.
\fIPOLICY\fP accepts \fBnone\fP, \fBauto\fP, \fBnearest\fP, or
\fBreversible\fP.
The default is \fBnone\fP. \fBauto\fP selects \fBnearest\fP,
with final-palette-only timing unless a timing override is supplied.
.br
Supported suboptions:
.br
\fBtiming\fP (short form \fBIVALUE\fP): \fBonce\fP,
\fBpolish\fP, \fBmerge\fP, \fBresolve\fP, or \fBall\fP.
Named wide-gamut output also accepts \fBsample\fP, \fBseed\fP,
\fBiteration\fP, \fBpremerge\fP, \fBmerge-iteration\fP,
and \fBapply\fP. The first five add their stage to final snap;
apply moves that final pass after cover, before lookup construction.
K-means owns the centroid seed/update stages; other solvers retain their
discrete representative algorithms. Sample snap changes only the training copy.
.br
\fBgrid\fP (short form \fBGVALUE\fP): destination candidate computation
\fBauto\fP, \fBdirect\fP, \fBtable\fP, \fBcache\fP, or
\fBfull\fP. These share the RGB101 to RGB8 to selected destination target model.
Automatic grid selection uses the small primary-contribution table.
The lazy cache adds 256 KiB, and the full grid uses about 23.6 MiB per build.
.br
\fBrate\fP (short form \fBAFACTOR\fP): floating value in 0.0-1.0.
.br
\fBchannel_l\fP (short form \fBLFACTOR\fP): floating value in
0.0-1.0.
.br
The corresponding \fBSIXEL_PALETTE_SNAP_*\fP environments provide defaults.
.TP 5
.B \-0 \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-clamp\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
limit byte and float spatial error-diffusion feedback. Values are \fBauto\fP, \fBsoft\fP,
\fBhard\fP and \fBnone\fP. The default \fBauto\fP selects \fBsoft\fP.
The policy bounds byte carry candidates and float working coordinates.
No policy promotes byte work to float.
This option does not clamp loader, CMS, resize or color-space conversions.
Original extended-range source coordinates remain intact.
.br
\fBsoft\fP scales the correction vector to fit the source-containing
working-coordinate bounds plus headroom. \fBhard\fP clips channels to the
source-containing bounds without headroom. \fBnone\fP leaves feedback open;
unrepresentable palette colors can accumulate growing error. Byte carry
overflow returns an error instead of wrapping.
.br
Suboption \fBthreshold\fP (short form \fBTFACTOR\fP) accepts a finite
value from 1 through 16; default 1.01. The soft margin per channel is
(threshold - 1) times that channel's nominal working-coordinate range.
The threshold is accepted but inactive with hard and none.
.br
\fBSIXEL_DITHER_CLAMP_POLICY\fP and \fBSIXEL_DITHER_CLAMP_THRESHOLD\fP
provide defaults. Explicit options take precedence. A repeated option
resets its suboptions before applying environment defaults again.
.TP 5
.B \-m \fIFILE\fP, \-\-mapfile=\fIFILE\fP
transform image colors to match this set of colors. Accepts image files
and palette files in Adobe Color Table (*.act), JASC/RIFF PAL (*.pal),
and GIMP Palette (*.gpl) formats. Use TYPE:PATH prefixes (act:, pal:,
pal\-jasc:, pal\-riff:, gpl:) to force a format. Without TYPE the
extension or file contents decide the palette type. Using TYPE:- reads
palette bytes from standard input (for example, gpl:-).
ACT/PAL/GPL entries are sRGB8 regardless of \fB-W\fP and \fB-U\fP.
Selected CPU float jobs retain unrounded working palettes for gamma
or Display P3 output. Byte-eligible gamma jobs retain their existing path.
Image mapfiles on the selected float path retain loader/CMS precision,
including when only the map requires float and the source remains byte-based.
This option is mutually exclusive with \fB-b\fP, \fB-e\fP, and
\fB-I\fP. Repeating \fB-m\fP is allowed; the last mapfile wins.
.TP 5
.B \-M \fIFILE\fP, \-\-mapfile\-output=\fIFILE\fP
export the prepared palette as sRGB8, independently of \fB-U\fP.
Colors outside sRGB are clipped at this file boundary. TYPE:PATH prefixes or file extensions (.act, .pal, .gpl)
select the output format. The .pal extension defaults to JASC text output;
use pal\-riff: for the RIFF layout. Writing to '-' requires TYPE:PATH;
TYPE:- directs the palette to standard output.
.TP 5
.B \-e, \-\-monochrome
output monochrome sixel image.
this option assumes the terminal background color is black.
This option is mutually exclusive with \fB-b\fP, \fB-m\fP, and
\fB-I\fP. Repeating \fB-e\fP is allowed.
It also conflicts with \fB--update-policy=delta\fP: the unpainted background
cannot erase foreground pixels from earlier frames under delta preservation.
.TP 5
.B \-k, \-\-insecure
skip HTTPS server certificate verification where supported.
Verification is normally enabled. Disabling it allows server impersonation,
interception of requests, and replacement of the downloaded image, even
though HTTPS still uses encryption. Use only for temporary diagnostics
against a controlled endpoint; repair the certificate or trust configuration
for normal use.
.IP
For an original URL beginning with lowercase https://, libcurl disables
certificate-chain and hostname verification. Native libfetch temporarily
sets SSL_NO_VERIFY_PEER and SSL_NO_VERIFY_HOSTNAME; their effect depends on
the linked implementation. An initial HTTP URL redirected to HTTPS, an
uppercase HTTPS scheme, or FTPS does not activate these two bindings' bypass.
WinHTTP requests ignoring unknown-CA, invalid-date, and hostname errors.
Emscripten ignores this option and retains runtime certificate policy.
This option does not supply a client certificate, disable SSH host-key
verification, or enable a missing protocol or TLS backend.
.TP 5
.B \-i, \-\-invert
assume the terminal background color is white.
make sense only when -e option is given.
.TP 5
.B \-I, \-\-high-color
output 15bpp sixel image
This option is mutually exclusive with \fB-b\fP, \fB-m\fP, and
\fB-e\fP. Repeating \fB-I\fP is allowed.
.TP 5
.B \-u, \-\-use-macro
use DECDMAC to define a static image or first-pass animation frames as
terminal macros, and DECINVM to invoke them. Later animation passes
reuse the stored definitions.
Requires a receiver that supports both SIXEL and terminal macros.
The loop policy is selected separately with \fB-l\fP.
Conflicts with \fB-Z delta\fP: the current macro emitter does not track
the base surface required by stored deltas. Select \fB-Z full\fP first.
See TERMINAL MACROS for preloading and receiver requirements.
.TP 5
.B \-n \fIMACRONO\fP, \-\-macro-number=\fIMACRONO\fP
select a fixed DECDMAC ID and suppress automatic invocation, even with
\fB-u\fP. MACRONO is a decimal integer from 0 through C INT_MAX;
the receiver may support a smaller range. Repeating this option selects
the last number. Multiple frames on the first pass redefine the same ID;
use \fB-S\fP to preload one frame from an animation.
Conflicts with \fB-Z delta\fP, including when \fB-u\fP is also present.
Select \fB-Z full\fP first to disable delta.
See TERMINAL MACROS for preloading examples and receiver requirements.
.TP 5
.B \-C \fICOMPLEXIONSCORE\fP, \-\-complexion-score=\fICOMPLEXIONSCORE\fP
[[deprecated]] accepted but ignored.
The ignored argument is not numerically validated.
.TP 5
.B \-g, \-\-ignore-delay
render GIF animation without delay.
.TP 5
.B \-S, \-\-static
render animated GIF as a static image.
.TP 5
.B \-d \fIDIFFUSION\fP, \-\-diffusion=\fIDIFFUSION\fP
choose diffusion method used with color reduction.
.br
common sub-option:
.br
\fBdeadzone=D\fP -> discard byte RGB corrections of Euclidean length below D
.br
\fBDD\fP -> compact form; default 5, 0 disables; finite nonnegative decimals
.br
Applies to auto, fs, atkinson, jajuni, stucki, burkes and sierra.
Float32 retains its existing behavior without a deadzone.
.br
\fBperturb=P\fP -> paired FS weight perturbation, 0.0-1.0 (default 0.0)
.br
\fBUP\fP -> short form of \fBperturb\fP
.br
\fBperturb_seed=S\fP -> signed 32-bit position seed (default 0)
.br
\fBRS\fP -> short form of \fBperturb_seed\fP
.br
These settings affect only FS, including auto when it resolves to FS.
Other methods accept and ignore them. Zero preserves the original output.
For example: \fBfs:perturb=0.5:scan=serpentine\fP.
.br
\fBband_overwrap=LINES\fP -> overlap adjacent parallel bands
(default depends on palette size)
.br
\fBOLINES\fP -> short form of \fBband_overwrap\fP
.br
\fBband_width=LINES\fP -> set the parallel dither band height
(rounded to a multiple of six)
.br
\fBBLINES\fP -> short form of \fBband_width\fP
.br
worker counts and affinity: see \fB\-=\fP/\fB\-\-threading\-policy\fP
.br
\fIDIFFUSION\fP is one of them:
.br
\fBauto\fP -> choose diffusion type automatically (default)
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBnone\fP -> do not diffuse
.br
\fBfs\fP -> Floyd-Steinberg method
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBatkinson\fP -> Bill Atkinson's method
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBjajuni\fP -> Jarvis, Judice & Ninke
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBstucki\fP -> Stucki's method
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBburkes\fP -> Burkes' method
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBsierra\fP -> Sierra diffusion family
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBvariant=LEVEL\fP -> \fB1\fP, \fB2\fP, or \fB3\fP (default \fB1\fP)
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBa_dither\fP -> positionally stable arithmetic dither
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBstrength=VALUE\fP -> fixed amplitude (unset: palette-size auto; 256 colors: 0.150)
.br
\fBTVALUE\fP -> short form of \fBstrength\fP
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBx_dither\fP -> positionally stable arithmetic xor based dither
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBstrength=VALUE\fP -> fixed amplitude (unset: palette-size auto; 256 colors: 0.100)
.br
\fBTVALUE\fP -> short form of \fBstrength\fP
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBbluenoise\fP -> tileable blue-noise ordered dither
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBstrength=VALUE\fP -> fixed amplitude (unset: palette-size auto; 256 colors: 0.055)
.br
\fBgradient_factor=VALUE\fP -> gamma for gradient attenuation (\fB<=0\fP disables; default 0)
.br
\fBGVALUE\fP -> short form of \fBgradient_factor\fP
.br
\fBphase=X,Y\fP -> phase offsets for blue-noise tile
.br
\fBseed=SEED\fP -> phase seed when phase is omitted
.br
\fBchannel=CHANNEL\fP -> \fBmono\fP or \fBrgb\fP (default mono)
.br
\fBsize=SIZE\fP -> \fB64\fP (embedded tile size)
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default raster)
.br
\fBlso2\fP -> libsixel variable error diffusion tables
.br
sub-option:
.br
\fBscan=SCANTYPE\fP -> \fBauto\fP, \fBraster\fP, or \fBserpentine\fP (default serpentine)
.br
Compact diffusion suboption names use one uppercase letter plus the value:
all methods scan=N, band_overwrap=O, band_width=B, deadzone=D,
perturb=U, perturb_seed=R;
sierra variant=V; a_dither/x_dither strength=T;
bluenoise strength=T, gradient_factor=G, phase=P, seed=S,
channel=C, size=Z.

.br
For a_dither, x_dither and bluenoise, an omitted strength selects automatic
amplitude by the actual paintable palette count K, excluding a reserved
omission key. For 2 through 256 colors, use
base + growth * (sqrt(256/K) - 1), with (base,growth) equal to
(0.150,0.100), (0.100,0.050) and (0.055,0.080), respectively.
One color uses zero automatic amplitude. Numeric strength fixes the amplitude
without scaling; the suboption overrides the environment. The same rule
applies to byte, float and GPU paths, in their existing working coordinates.
This is a simple heuristic, not an image-specific quality optimum.
.TP 5
.B \-f \fIFINDTYPE\fP, \-\-find\-largest=\fIFINDTYPE\fP
choose the Heckbert split direction; an explicit -p is not required.
Auto normally resolves to norm. Norm compares native channel ranges with
ratios CIELAB 2:1:1, DIN99d 2:1:1, and RGB/linear RGB/OKLab 1:1:1.
Lum uses BT.601 (0.2989:0.5866:0.1145) for RGB and linear RGB;
CIELAB, DIN99d and OKLab use 4:1:1, 4:1:1 and 2:1:1 respectively.
Pca uses the first principal component of nominal histogram coordinates.
The Heckbert weights suboption overrides norm/lum ratios with positive finite
native-coordinate L/A/B ratios; a common multiplier cancels. Auto restores
the color-space defaults. The cut suboption chooses the minimum weighted within-child squared error (sse, default)
or the weighted median. Order chooses the next
box by largest within-box squared error (sse, default) or population.
SSE uses squared, range-compensated axis weights in all channels; it permits
singleton outliers. PCA retains its projection geometry and uses these
weights only for the SSE objective. All profiles inherit sse/sse unless explicitly overridden.
.TP 5
.B \-s \fISELECTTYPE\fP, \-\-select\-color=\fISELECTTYPE\fP
choose each final Heckbert cluster representative, including after Ward
merging and Lloyd reassignment. An explicit -p is not required.
Center is the midpoint of member channel extrema; average is the unweighted
mean of distinct retained histogram colors; histogram is their
occurrence-weighted mean. Byte representatives round to nearest with halves
rounded up. Extended float representatives remain fractional.
Auto uses average with any enabled dither and histogram with none, including
after Ward and Lloyd. Auto diffusion resolves to FS above 16 colors and
Atkinson otherwise; both are enabled dithers. Positional
dithering also uses average. Other solvers retain their representatives with auto;
explicit center, average and histogram are rejected for kmeans, medoids and
center solvers. Ward and Lloyd still use weighted centroids internally.
Structured external palettes (-m), builtin palettes (-b), preserved PAL8
input and reused palettes bypass this control. Image mapfiles that construct
a palette follow the solver contract. Later cover/snap policies still apply.
.TP 5
.B \-c \fIREGION\fP, \-\-crop=\fIREGION\fP
crop source image to fit the specified geometry.
.br
REGION should be formatted as '%dx%d+%d+%d'.
.TP 5
.B \-w \fIWIDTH\fP, \-\-width=\fIWIDTH\fP
resize image to specified width.
.br
\fIWIDTH\fP is represented by the following syntax:
.br
auto       -> preserving aspect ratio (default)
.br
<number>%  -> scale width with given percentage
.br
<number>   -> scale width with pixel counts
.br
<number>c  -> scale width with terminal cell count
.br
<number>px -> scale width with pixel counts
.TP 5
.B \-h \fIHEIGHT\fP, \-\-height=\fIHEIGHT\fP
resize image to specified height.
.br
\fIHEIGHT\fP is represented by the following syntax
.br
auto       -> preserving aspect ratio (default)
.br
<number>%  -> scale height with given percentage
.br
<number>   -> scale height with pixel counts
.br
<number>c  -> scale height with terminal cell count
.br
<number>px -> scale height with pixel counts
.TP 5
.B \-r \fIRESAMPLINGTYPE\fP, \-\-resampling=\fIRESAMPLINGTYPE\fP
choose resampling method used with -w or -h option (scaling).
.br
\fIRESAMPLINGTYPE\fP is one of them:
.br
nearest  -> Nearest-Neighbor method
.br
gaussian -> Gaussian filter
.br
hanning  -> Hanning filter
.br
hamming  -> Hamming filter
.br
bilinear -> Bilinear filter (default)
.br
welsh    -> Welsh filter
.br
bicubic  -> Bicubic filter
.br
lanczos2 -> Lanczos-2 filter
.br
lanczos3 -> Lanczos-3 filter
.br
lanczos4 -> Lanczos-4 filter
.TP 5
.B \-q \fIQUALITYMODE\fP, \-\-quality=\fIQUALITYMODE\fP
select quality of color quanlization.
.br
\fIQUALITYMODE\fP is one of them:
.br
auto -> decide quality mode automatically (default)
.br
high -> high quality and low speed mode
.br
low  -> low quality and high speed mode
.br
full -> quality and careful speed mode
.TP 5
.B \-\(ti \fILOOKUPPOLICY\fP, \-\-lookup\-policy=\fILOOKUPPOLICY\fP
choose histogram lookup width and backend.
.br
\fILOOKUPPOLICY\fP is one of them:
.br
auto      -> exact VP-tree lookup (default)
.br
5bit      -> force classic 5-bit buckets
.br
           sub-option:
.br
             packing=linear|morton|hilbert, short form Pvalue
.br
             shared_instance=0|1, short form S0 or S1
             (0: worker-local, 1: shared)
.br
6bit      -> force 6-bit RGB buckets (RGB inputs only)
.br
           sub-option:
.br
             packing=linear|morton|hilbert, short form Pvalue
.br
             shared_instance=0|1, short form S0 or S1
             (0: worker-local, 1: shared)
.br
none      -> disable LUT caching and scan the palette directly
.br
certlut -> hierarchical LUT with center-based dominance testing
.br
          sub-option:
.br
            shared_instance=0|1, short form S0 or S1
            (0: worker-local, 1: shared)
.br
          The RGB cube is divided into 64x64x64 cells and refined only
          where necessary.  certlut reuses a single kd-tree built from
          the palette to find the nearest and second nearest colours at
          each cell centre.  A pairwise bound either accepts the centre
          winner for the cell or causes further refinement.
.br
eytzinger -> implicit binary tree lookup with a small neighbour scan.
.br
fhedt    -> Voronoi grid built via a 3-pass 3D EDT with optional
           boundary refinement at cell corners.
.br
           sub-options:
.br
             resolution=64|128|256, short form Rvalue
.br
             refine=0|1, short form F0 or F1
.br
             shared=0|1, short form S0 or S1
.br
             dist2=0|1, short form D0 or D1
.br
             cache=0|1, short form C0 or C1
.br
             tile_xy=1..1024, short form Xvalue
.br
             tile_depth=1..1024, short form Tvalue
.br
             first_touch=0|1, short form O0 or O1
.br
             worker affinity: \fB\-= *:pin=0|1\fP
.br
delaunay -> Exact Delaunay graph walk; lowest index at distance ties.
.br
blockcull -> Exact scan after culling colors that cannot be nearest
             anywhere in a pixel block; lowest index at distance ties.
.br
vptree  -> VP-tree lookup built from palette entries with safe-distance
           pruning.
.br
rbc     -> Random Ball Cover cluster pruning.
.br
mahalanobis -> exhaustive normalized scan in RBC cluster order.
.PP
When shared_instance is present on the command line, it overrides
SIXEL_LOOKUP_5BIT_SHARED_INSTANCE,
SIXEL_LOOKUP_6BIT_SHARED_INSTANCE, and
SIXEL_LOOKUP_CERTLUT_SHARED_INSTANCE.
.PP
The \fInone\fP policy skips the lookup caches entirely so each pixel
comparison scans the palette directly.  This mirrors the behaviour that
`-q full` previously forced implicitly.
.PP
The lookup policies honour the following environment variables.  A matching
command-line sub-option takes precedence:
.br
SIXEL_DITHER_LOOKUP_POLICY (auto, 5bit, 6bit, none, certlut, eytzinger,
fhedt, vptree, delaunay, blockcull, rbc, or mahalanobis; default auto)
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_PACKING (linear, morton, or hilbert; default linear) selects
dense LUT packing for 5bit/6bit lookups; other policies ignore it.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBpacking=VALUE\fP (short form \fBPVALUE\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_RESOLUTION (64, 128, or 256; default 64)
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBresolution=VALUE\fP (short form \fBRVALUE\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_WEIGHTS (auto or L/A/B; default auto) chooses
FHEDT channel ratios. The matching sub-option is \fBweights=VALUE\fP
(short form \fBWVALUE\fP, for example \fBW3/1/1\fP or \fBWauto\fP).
The auto ratios are 3/1/1 for CIELAB and OKLab, 2/1/1 for DIN99d,
and 1/1/1 for RGB/linear RGB. Axes follow the working colorspace (-W):
R/G/B in gamma or linear RGB, L/a/b in perceptual spaces.
Explicit ratios are three positive finite
numbers; 1/1/1 disables extra weighting. They scale nominal-range-normalized
coordinate differences before squaring. A common scale has no effect.
Grid construction, refinement and exterior queries use the same metric.
Nonuniform ratios require float32 lookup; the byte path retains equal
weights. Ratios that underflow the normalized float32 coefficients are
rejected. This changes lookup distance, not colorspace conversion.
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_REFINE (0 or 1; default 1) enables corner refinement
for boundary cells.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBrefine=0|1\fP (short form \fBF0|F1\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_USE_DIST2 (0 or 1; default 0 because recent measurements did
not show a consistent win) keeps per-voxel EDT distances so boundary voxels can
skip refinement when the center is already safely inside the Voronoi region.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBdist2=0|1\fP (short form \fBD0|D1\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_USE_CACHE (0 or 1; default 0 because the measured gain was
negligible; enable explicitly to re-evaluate) enables a small per-thread cache
keyed by voxel coordinates to speed up repeated lookups.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBcache=0|1\fP (short form \fBC0|C1\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_SHARED (0 or 1; default 1) builds the grid once
after the working palette is finalized and shares it read-only by reference.
An asynchronous palette job builds it within its thread budget while full-frame
preprocessing can continue. Cache entries remain thread-local. Setting 0
retains independent grid construction for diagnostics and comparisons.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBshared=0|1\fP (short form \fBS0|S1\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_CERTLUT_SHARED_INSTANCE (0 or 1; default 0) toggles whether
CERTLUT caches are shared across worker threads or rebuilt per worker
when \-\-lookup-policy does not set shared_instance explicitly.
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_5BIT_SHARED_INSTANCE (0 or 1; default 1) controls whether the
5bit dense LUT is shared across workers without locks when
\-\-lookup-policy does not set shared_instance explicitly.
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_6BIT_SHARED_INSTANCE (0 or 1; default 1) controls whether the
6bit dense LUT is shared across workers without locks when
\-\-lookup-policy does not set shared_instance explicitly.
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_TILE_XY (adaptive default; clamped to the image size and
falling back to the adaptive choice on invalid input) sets the tile
width/height for the EDT passes.  The adaptive default shrinks tiles for
diverse palettes and grows them when the palette is biased to reduce
scheduling overhead.  Legacy SIXEL_FHEDT_TILE_XY remains accepted for
compatibility.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBtile_xy=VALUE\fP (short form \fBXVALUE\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_TILE_DEPTH (adaptive default; same clamping as
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_TILE_XY) sets the tile depth for the EDT passes and follows
the same adaptive policy.  Legacy SIXEL_FHEDT_TILE_DEPTH remains accepted for
compatibility.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBtile_depth=VALUE\fP (short form \fBTVALUE\fP).
.br
SIXEL_LOOKUP_FHEDT_FIRST_TOUCH (0 or 1; default 0) zeroes FHEDT tiles in
parallel before the EDT to let NUMA systems place pages on the worker that will
use them.  Legacy SIXEL_FHEDT_FIRST_TOUCH remains accepted for compatibility.
.br
The matching sub-option is \fBfirst_touch=0|1\fP
(short form \fBO0|O1\fP).
.br
FHEDT worker affinity follows \fB\-= *:pin=0|1\fP (default 0 for FHEDT);
pinning errors are ignored so portability is preserved.
.br
SIXEL_LOG_PATH writes the shared JSON timeline log consumed by
tools/timeline.py
when set; unset leaves logging disabled. The \fB\-J/\-\-log\-path\fP option
takes precedence.
.br
SIXEL_LOG_LINES controls per-line logging when
SIXEL_LOG_PATH is set. Unset or empty disables line events. A positive
integer logs every Nth line; legacy zero, negative, and invalid values log
every line. The matching diagnostics sub-option is
\fBlog_lines=1..2147483647\fP (short form \fBNVALUE\fP).
.TP 5
.B \-l \fILOOPMODE\fP, \-\-loop\-control=\fILOOPMODE\fP
select loop control mode for GIF animation.
.br
auto    -> honer the setting of GIF header (default)
.br
force   -> always enable loop
.br
disable -> always disable loop
.TP 5
.B \-T \fIFRAME_NO\fP, \-\-start-frame=\fIFRAME_NO\fP
set the animation start frame index for supported loaders.
.br
0 or positive values select absolute frame indices.
.br
negative values count from the end (-1 selects the last frame).
.TP 5
.B \-t \fIPALETTETYPE\fP, \-\-palette\-type=\fIPALETTETYPE\fP
select palette color space type.
.br
auto -> choose palette type automatically (default)
.br
hls  -> use HLS color space
.br
rgb  -> use RGB color space
.TP 5
.B \-b \fIBUILTINPALETTE\fP, \-\-builtin\-palette=\fIBUILTINPALETTE\fP
select built-in palette type
.br
xterm16    -> X default 16 color map
.br
xterm256   -> X default 256 color map
.br
vt340mono  -> VT340 monochrome map
.br
vt340color -> VT340 color map
.br
gray1      -> 1bit grayscale map
.br
gray2      -> 2bit grayscale map
.br
gray4      -> 4bit grayscale map
.br
gray8      -> 8bit grayscale map
.br
oklch-lattice256 -> OKLCh lattice 256color map
.br
This option is mutually exclusive with \fB-m\fP, \fB-e\fP, and
\fB-I\fP. Repeating \fB-b\fP is allowed; the last built-in palette wins.
.TP 5
.B \-E \fIENCODEPOLICY\fP, \-\-encode\-policy=\fIENCODEPOLICY\fP
select encoding policy
.br
auto -> choose encoding policy automatically (default)
.br
fast -> encode as fast as possible
.br
size -> encode to as small sixel sequence as possible
.TP 5
.B \-B \fIBGCOLOR\fP, \-\-bgcolor=\fIBGCOLOR\fP
.br
specify background color
.br
\fIBGCOLOR\fP is represented by the following syntax
.br
#rgb
.br
#rrggbb
.br
#rrrgggbbb
.br
#rrrrggggbbbb
.br
rgb:r/g/b
.br
rgb:rr/gg/bb
.br
rgb:rrr/ggg/bbb
.br
rgb:rrrr/gggg/bbbb
.br
Hexadecimal digits are case-insensitive. Each component keeps its most
significant eight bits without rounding; a single digit is zero-filled
in the low nibble (for example, #123 means RGB 16,32,48).
.TP 5
.B \-N \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-background\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
choose the background source used for alpha composition.
.br
file_first -> prefer PNG bKGD or the GIF logical-screen background (default).
.br
explicit_first -> prefer \fB\-B\fP, \fBSIXEL_BGCOLOR\fP, or an OSC 11 reply.
.br
An explicit loader \fBbackground_policy\fP suboption overrides this option.
This option overrides \fBSIXEL_BACKGROUND_POLICY\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-A \fIALPHAPOLICY\fP, \-\-alpha\-policy=\fIALPHAPOLICY\fP
choose source alpha and omitted SIXEL pixel policy
.br
auto -> use clear normally and keep for transparent offset or 6delta output
(default).
.br
composite -> composite source alpha over the resolved background.
Sources are \fB-B\fP, OSC 11, and supported file backgrounds. If unresolved,
fall back to keep.
.br
clear -> preserve alpha-zero and emit DCS P2=0, requesting the terminal to
clear omitted pixels.
.br
keep -> preserve alpha-zero and emit DCS P2=1, requesting the terminal to keep
existing pixels.
.br
Semi-transparent pixels are composited when a background is available because
SIXEL cannot represent partial alpha. P2 rendering details are
terminal-dependent.
.TP 5
.B \-+ \fILEFT,TOP\fP, \-\-transparent\-offset=\fILEFT,TOP\fP
add transparent left/top pixel offset with DCS P2=1 image-plane reuse.
0,0 disables the offset. Requires
\fB--alpha-policy=auto\fP or \fBkeep\fP. Incompatible with \fB-O\fP and
\fB-I\fP.
.TP 5
.B \-Z \fIPOLICY\fP, \-\-update\-policy=\fIPOLICY\fP
Select full output (default), retained-plane delta updates or progressive still presentation.
\fBfull\fP disables temporal keeps and discards retained history, while preserving source-alpha handling.
\fBdelta\fP enables 6delta with optional \fB:threshold=0..255\fP (short form :Tvalue) and \fB:error=diffuse|skip\fP (short form :Evalue).
Defaults are threshold=0, error=diffuse and progressive=0; full accepts no suboptions.
The delta suboption \fB:residual=0|1\fP (:Rvalue, default 0) lowers the early-keep threshold in persistent residual cells without sending extra packets. Use \fB-Zdelta:R1:T8\fP.
\fB:threshold_metric=auto|max|rgb241\fP (:Dvalue, default auto) selects the early-keep metric. Auto resolves to max for R0 and rgb241 for R1. Max uses the greatest component difference; rgb241 compares (2*dR*dR+4*dG*dG+dB*dB)/7 with T*T in gamma RGB8 units.
\fB:residual_threshold=0..255\fP (:Hvalue, finite, default 1.5) is the strict threshold on signed Gaussian-filtered residual magnitude, not Delta E. \fB:residual_keep_threshold=0..255\fP (:Kvalue, integer, default 0) caps T in selected cells. \fB:residual_frames=1..255\fP (:Fvalue, integer, default 3) counts consecutive successful input observations.
The mask uses sigma 4 pixels, radius 12, and up to 64 by 64 screen cells. History commits only after successful output. Source drops do not count; frame numbers need not be consecutive. No extra work runs after the last input.
R1 requires full-plane opaque gamma RGB8 targets, RGB output and none, a_dither, x_dither or bluenoise application; CPU and Metal use the same mask. R1 conflicts with P1 and explicit max metric. Other workspaces, float targets, alpha holes and partial updates are unsupported. R0 preserves ordinary delta behavior.
Each -Z selection resets its suboptions to defaults, environment, then explicit suffixes. R1 alone preserves the default T0, which already bypasses approximate early keeps.

The delta suboption \fB:progressive=1|0\fP (short form :P1 or :P0) pairs ordinary animation updates with at most one non-overlapping repair image per source interval, using an independent residual palette.
The repair excludes positions painted by that ordinary update, uses the latest source and prioritizes luminance structure. Both images share one source delay and the available time/output budget; there is no combined palette or extra playback delay.
It preserves absolute source-frame deadlines and refines the final frame after finite playback.
\fB-g\fP and zero-duration frames skip intermediate repair. Thread-disabled builds send pairs synchronously.
Host output acceptance does not acknowledge terminal presentation; blocking host writes cannot guarantee a hard frame deadline.
\fBprogressive\fP selects one composed still from any input and repeatedly overlays sparse repairs until convergence, a representable-color stall or a resource limit.
Both progressive and delta accept \fB:stop_threshold=auto|0..1e154\fP (short form :Svalue, default auto), \fB:max_passes=0..4294967295\fP (:Nvalue, default 0), and \fB:max_seconds=SECONDS\fP (:Mvalue, default 5).
Stop threshold is a distance in the selected \fB-W\fP workspace, not Delta E00. Auto selects a workspace-dependent target.
Seconds must be finite and nonnegative. Zero disables the respective pass or time cap; a zero stop threshold requests exact agreement.
Passes include the initial ordinary image and accepted repair packets; work-budget yields do not count.
Elapsed time starts when the ordinary image is accepted and includes source-binding work. Each animation source gets new limits; the final-frame tail retains its source's elapsed time and pass count.
Resource limits return success with the last complete image and do not certify quality. \fB-v\fP reports converged, stalled, max_passes or max_seconds. An admitted packet always completes, so a blocked write can exceed the time limit.
These controls do not enable delta repair without progressive=1. For example, \fB-Z progressive:Sauto:N10:M2\fP permits at most ten total passes and two seconds after the initial image.
Animation input defaults to frame zero, excluding an APNG poster; \fB-T\fP overrides the source-index environment setting.
This implicit still selection does not persist after another \fB-Z\fP selection.
Each capture saves the initial cursor once and restores it only for actual later packets.
Decode such a still capture with \fBsixel2png -M 6delta-progressive\fP or assess it with \fBlsqa -M 6delta-progressive\fP.
Progressive repair requires generated palettes. Metal can accelerate residual selection, global sampling and exhaustive palette lookup; palette construction, paint acceptance and convergence auditing remain on the CPU. GPU auto admission uses the unresolved population for selection and the selected population for lookup against palette_threshold; force requires an available Metal backend. The ordinary baseline retains its own GPU input and dither restrictions. Progressive conflicts with fixed palettes, high color, multiplexer penetration and transparent offsets, in addition to delta's restrictions.
Direct frame and byte APIs remain caller-paced; option selection alone never starts repair.
A valid retained RGB pixel is kept before lookup when every channel differs by at most the threshold; at 0 this requires an exact match.
Otherwise, a pixel is kept when the retained color is at least as close to the compared sample as the selected palette color, using squared RGB distance.
Larger thresholds trade freshness for fewer lookups; 255 keeps every valid retained RGB888 pixel.
Diffuse propagates the error from retained RGB; skip omits kept-pixel diffusion.
Delta requires \fB--alpha-policy=auto\fP or \fBkeep\fP.
It conflicts with \fB-e\fP, whose unpainted background cannot erase earlier
foreground pixels. It also conflicts with \fB-O\fP: OR mode's P2=5
does not carry delta's omitted-pixel preservation. DRCS output via \fB-@\fP also conflicts because glyph definitions do not update the retained display plane. Opaque black-and-white output via \fB-b gray1\fP can
paint both colors and remains supported.
Macro output (\fB-u\fP or \fB-n\fP) also conflicts: definitions need not be invoked, and later invocations may have a different base surface.
PNG snapshot destinations also conflict: the standalone decoder cannot
reconstruct omitted pixels without the retained base.
History must match the receiver's display and frame placement.
